<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">António da Silva Porto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1817–1890</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">António Francisco Ferreira da Silva <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> was a Portuguese trader and explorer who settled at Belmonte in the Viye (Bié) kingdom of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> in the 1840s and led caravans deep into the interior to trade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, wax and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>. In 1890, after King Ndunduma turned against a Portuguese expedition, he took his own life by setting off a keg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>. The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuito">Kuito</a> was long named Silva Porto after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-da-silva-porto.jpg" alt="António da Silva Porto" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ant%C3%B3nio_Francisco_Ferreira_da_Silva_Porto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
